==English==
{{was wotd|2007|July|6}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰewh₂-}}
From {{bor|en|frm|obfusquer}}, from {{der|en|fro|offusquer}}, and the participle stem of {{der|en|LL.|obfuscō}}, from {{der|en|la|ob-}} + {{m|la|fuscō||to darken}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɒbfʌskeɪt/|/ˈɒbfəskeɪt/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɑbfʌskeɪt/|/ˈɑbfəskeɪt/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-obfuscate.ogg|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To make [[dark]]; to [[overshadow]].
# To [[deliberately]] make more [[confusing]] in order to [[conceal]] the truth.
#: {{ux|en|'''obfuscate''' facts}}
#: {{ux|en|Can weakness be really '''obfuscated'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|Before leaving the scene, the murderer set a fire in order to '''obfuscate''' any evidence of his identity.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Anonymous White House Official|title=White House reels as FBI director contradicts official claims about alleged abuser|journal=Washington Post|date=February 13 2018|titleurl=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/white-house-reels-as-fbi-director-contradicts-official-claims-about-alleged-abuser/2018/02/13/f010f256-10d9-11e8-9570-29c9830535e5_story.html?utm_term=.a8b554327809|passage=When asked if Kelly could have been more transparent or truthful, that official wrote: “In this White House, it’s simply not in our DNA. Truthful and transparent is great, but we don’t even have a coherent strategy to '''obfuscate'''.”}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} To [[alter]] code while preserving its behavior but [[conceal]]ing its structure and intent.
#: {{ux|en|We need to '''obfuscate''' these classes before we ship the final release.}}

